    "step8_outcome_verification": {
      "primary_intent": "Identify the most recent stable (non-preview/pre-release) Playwright release from the official microsoft/playwright GitHub Releases page, report its tag and publication date, summarize three concrete release-note changes from that release, and corroborate one of those changes using official Playwright documentation. A reasonable user would be flexible on minor wording/formatting, but not on the correctness of the release date or whether the chosen release is actually the newest stable one.",
      "reasoning": "Nitpicks: Minor summarization/phrasing differences in the three changes (as long as they map to real release-note items) would be acceptable; also not providing a URL for the GitHub release is not required.\n\nCritical issues: The publication date the agent reported is wrong. The agent output claims the v1.63.0 publication date is 2025-01-07, but DOM evidence on the repository page shows “v1.63.0 Latest Sep 4, 2026” (DOM state 0 and 6), and the release page shows “released this 04 Sep 22:40” (states 1–2), which contradicts 2025-01-07. Because the task explicitly requires the publication date as part of the deliverable, this is a substantive factual error.\n\nWhat was completed correctly: The agent did navigate to the correct GitHub Releases page without signing in (state 1) and selected v1.63.0 marked “Latest,” which strongly indicates it is the newest release in the UI. The agent also provided three changes that are supported across the GitHub v1.63.0 release page DOM snapshots (e.g., “Test locks,” “Locate across frames,” and “Visible-only locators” appear across states 2 and 3), and it successfully verified at least one change (“Test locks”) using the official Playwright docs release notes (state 9).\n\nOverall outcome: Although most parts of the task are satisfied (correct repo/releases navigation, correct release tag, three real changes, and an official-doc verification), the explicit requirement to report the publication date is not met due to a clear contradiction with the DOM. An average user would not consider the task complete with an incorrect date in the core metadata.",
      "output_success": false
    },
